  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1121 AM MDT Tue Jul 28 2026\n\nWARM WITH PM STORMS THROUGH WEDNESDAY: \n\nWith a stagnant weather pattern in place, conditons remain \nlargely unchanged today. Scattered afternoon/evening showers and\nstorms develop once again, with heavy rainfall (flash flooding)\nbeing the primary threat due to slow storm motions. Lightning, \nsmall hail, and gusty outflow winds up to 45 mph will also be \npossible. The CAMs support isolated convection contiuing \nthrought the overnight hours, similar to what we saw early this \nmorning.\n\nTomorrow, a shortwave trough will move through the region. This\nwill provide enhanced lift and support scattered to widespread \nconvection as it moves through. It should also help speed up \nstorm motions, but localized flash flooding can't be ruled out \nover burn scars and other sensitive terrain. \n\nLATE WEEK PATTERN CHANGE: \n\nBehind the aformentioned shortwave, dry air beings to filter \nback into the region. It will take a few days before residual \nmoisture is completely scrubbed from the atmosphere, so we \nexpect some convection Thursday and Friday with coverage \ndecreaseing each day. As things dry out, the primary threat of \nthunderstorms will switch from heavy rainfall to gusty outflow \nwinds up to 50 mph. \n\nHigh pressure sets up over Utah this weekend bringing hot and \nmostly dry conditions. Temperatures will run around 10 degrees \nabove normal, but with less moisture in the air, you can expect \nmore of a \"dry heat\" compared to what we've endured lately.",
